(typeof module==="object"&&module.exports?require("../curriculum.js"):BodyguardCurriculum).register(10,[["q10-34e7ff8a9562","s10.0-a0be188e3a92","10.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstriated muscle found in the heart; joined to one another at intercalated discs and under the regulation of pacemaker cells, which contract as one unit to pump blood through the circulatory system. Cardiac muscle is under involuntary control.","cardiac muscle",["smooth muscle","muscle tension","synaptic cleft"],"cardiac muscle: striated muscle found in the heart; joined to one another at intercalated discs and under the regulation of pacemaker cells, which contract as one unit to pump blood through the circulatory system. Cardiac muscle is under involuntary control..","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","eip-id1165744650424","f-287473820780"],["q10-3580f40d1129","s10.0-ac8bdc808a8f","10.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nforce generated by the contraction of the muscle; tension generated during isotonic contractions and isometric contractions","muscle tension",["cardiac muscle","smooth muscle","muscle tone"],"muscle tension: force generated by the contraction of the muscle; tension generated during isotonic contractions and isometric contractions.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","eip-id1165744650424","f-05d07f5fc0c6"],["q10-c1ea8ea2343c","s10.0-ca50810dea8c","10.0","Introduction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nnonstriated, mononucleated muscle in the skin that is associated with hair follicles; assists in moving materials in the walls of internal organs, blood vessels, and internal passageways","smooth muscle",["cardiac muscle","muscle tension","action potential"],"smooth muscle: nonstriated, mononucleated muscle in the skin that is associated with hair follicles; assists in moving materials in the walls of internal organs, blood vessels, and internal passageways.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","eip-id1165744650424","f-63cf79f66744"],["q10-d80f4a64596e","s10.0-a0be188e3a92","10.0","Introduction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____, found in the heart, is concerned with pumping blood through the circulatory system.","Cardiac muscle",["muscle tension","smooth muscle","latent period"],"Cardiac muscle, found in the heart, is concerned with pumping blood through the circulatory system.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1574141","f-6d4ab20092ac"],["q10-1a3cfb5fd730","s10.1-0de11d6c6b9b","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprotein that makes up most of the thin myofilaments in a sarcomere muscle fiber","actin",["troponin","myosin","tropomyosin"],"actin: protein that makes up most of the thin myofilaments in a sarcomere muscle fiber.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1388147","f-edaeee44c0a9"],["q10-8a2e10d7dbb5","s10.1-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nchange in voltage of a cell membrane in response to a stimulus that results in transmission of an electrical signal; unique to neurons and muscle fibers","action potential",["cardiac muscle","skeletal muscle","smooth muscle"],"action potential: change in voltage of a cell membrane in response to a stimulus that results in transmission of an electrical signal; unique to neurons and muscle fibers.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id1840923","f-8685547cde46"],["q10-91c4f87249a4","s10.1-6d5975609ea3","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nability to shorten (contract) forcibly","contractility",["excitability","troponin","tropomyosin"],"contractility: ability to shorten (contract) forcibly.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2017239","f-45b119c0eaf9"],["q10-0d19c666841e","s10.1-d8f9e9ada49f","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nability to stretch and rebound","elasticity",["actin","troponin","tropomyosin"],"elasticity: ability to stretch and rebound.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id2017239","f-11abbb26ecac"],["q10-69facf7f9f58","s10.1-ace27ef201ba","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nability to undergo neural stimulation","excitability",["actin","tropomyosin","elasticity"],"excitability: ability to undergo neural stimulation.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1840923","f-b47b7de90f05"],["q10-f6b369e4b7a7","s10.1-de777c7e646e","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nability to lengthen (extend)","extensibility",["contractility","actin","troponin"],"extensibility: ability to lengthen (extend).","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id2017239","f-c12c1c74b35c"],["q10-4dd63a33d8e8","s10.1-5479a81b0f86","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprotein that makes up most of the thick cylindrical myofilament within a sarcomere muscle fiber","myosin",["troponin","excitability","extensibility"],"myosin: protein that makes up most of the thick cylindrical myofilament within a sarcomere muscle fiber.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1388147","f-5a31f220300d"],["q10-bff4e6e742c3","s10.1-41d82176e2d1","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstriated, multinucleated muscle that requires signaling from the nervous system to trigger contraction; most skeletal muscles are referred to as voluntary muscles that move bones and produce movement","skeletal muscle",["cardiac muscle","smooth muscle","action potential"],"skeletal muscle: striated, multinucleated muscle that requires signaling from the nervous system to trigger contraction; most skeletal muscles are referred to as voluntary muscles that move bones and produce movement.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id1840923","f-575229ef56fd"],["q10-752bf4aa5278","s10.1-621e2b6031eb","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregulatory protein that covers myosin-binding sites to prevent actin from binding to myosin","tropomyosin",["troponin","contractility","extensibility"],"tropomyosin: regulatory protein that covers myosin-binding sites to prevent actin from binding to myosin.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1388147","f-8a4b77b368cb"],["q10-15aefc4e279b","s10.1-6bb33b4e7fea","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregulatory protein that binds to actin, tropomyosin, and calcium","troponin",["excitability","extensibility","myosin"],"troponin: regulatory protein that binds to actin, tropomyosin, and calcium.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1388147","f-7a7435ed29c2"],["q10-2f62fc8c9801","s10.1-41d82176e2d1","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMuscle is one of the four primary tissue types of the body, and the body contains three types of muscle tissue: ____, cardiac muscle, and smooth muscle.","skeletal muscle",["action potential","excitability","tropomyosin"],"Muscle is one of the four primary tissue types of the body, and the body contains three types of muscle tissue: skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and smooth muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1840923","f-32fb8ec4b90f"],["q10-e881c6de84fa","s10.1-41d82176e2d1","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhile the nervous system can influence the excitability of cardiac and smooth muscle to some degree, ____ completely depends on signaling from the nervous system to work properly.","skeletal muscle",["cardiac muscle","action potential","elasticity"],"While the nervous system can influence the excitability of cardiac and smooth muscle to some degree, skeletal muscle completely depends on signaling from the nervous system to work properly.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1840923","f-b0c9b7189b2d"],["q10-e0f0dadf51a3","s10.1-5479a81b0f86","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe muscles all begin the actual process of contracting (shortening) when a protein called actin is pulled by a protein called ____.","myosin",["excitability","contractility","elasticity"],"The muscles all begin the actual process of contracting (shortening) when a protein called actin is pulled by a protein called myosin.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1388147","f-941e9c604f2c"],["q10-e35ef5f56b33","s10.1-41d82176e2d1","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe actin and myosin proteins are arranged very regularly in the cytoplasm of individual muscle cells (referred to as fibers) in both ____ and cardiac muscle, which creates a pattern, or stripes, called striations.","skeletal muscle",["action potential","smooth muscle","tropomyosin"],"The actin and myosin proteins are arranged very regularly in the cytoplasm of individual muscle cells (referred to as fibers) in both skeletal muscle and cardiac muscle, which creates a pattern, or stripes, called striations.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2020397","f-14ba8e6da9e8"],["q10-27c12eaf03fe","s10.1-a0be188e3a92","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ fibers each have one to two nuclei and are physically and electrically connected to each other so that the entire heart contracts as one unit (called a syncytium).","cardiac muscle",["smooth muscle","skeletal muscle","action potential"],"Cardiac muscle fibers each have one to two nuclei and are physically and electrically connected to each other so that the entire heart contracts as one unit (called a syncytium).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2020397","f-071b433fa9c3"],["q10-479b3cb3ac30","s10.1-ca50810dea8c","10.1","Overview of Muscle Tissues","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe less organized appearance of ____ should not be interpreted as less efficient.","smooth muscle",["action potential","skeletal muscle","cardiac muscle"],"However, the less organized appearance of smooth muscle should not be interpreted as less efficient.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2336777","f-1865326a65db"],["q10-af868594af36","s10.2-646e27bbccea","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nneurotransmitter that binds at a motor end-plate to trigger depolarization","acetylcholine (ACh)",["motor unit","thick filament","skeletal muscle"],"acetylcholine (ACh): neurotransmitter that binds at a motor end-plate to trigger depolarization.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id2023666","f-4f7904219722"],["q10-7a5eb5364661","s10.2-fcafb2752ed0","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nbroad, tendon-like sheet of connective tissue that attaches a skeletal muscle to another skeletal muscle or to a bone","aponeurosis",["sarcolemma","sarcomere","neurotransmitter"],"aponeurosis: broad, tendon-like sheet of connective tissue that attaches a skeletal muscle to another skeletal muscle or to a bone.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2052461","f-8d0d7aea93da"],["q10-4d7ef7d850ed","s10.2-9ab550399a2d","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nto reduce the voltage difference between the inside and outside of a cell’s plasma membrane (the sarcolemma for a muscle fiber), making the inside less negative than at rest","depolarize",["endomysium","aponeurosis","neurotransmitter"],"depolarize: to reduce the voltage difference between the inside and outside of a cell’s plasma membrane (the sarcolemma for a muscle fiber), making the inside less negative than at rest.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id2023666","f-b346f25a1e13"],["q10-77121b36f5ff","s10.2-f7c2d128ff2c","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloose, and well-hydrated connective tissue covering each muscle fiber in a skeletal muscle","endomysium",["perimysium","actin","aponeurosis"],"endomysium: loose, and well-hydrated connective tissue covering each muscle fiber in a skeletal muscle.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fig-ch10_02_01","f-2dfb5ad60adc"],["q10-127505da852e","s10.2-8fdacda63194","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nouter layer of connective tissue around a skeletal muscle","epimysium",["tropomyosin","fascicle","T-tubule"],"epimysium: outer layer of connective tissue around a skeletal muscle.","The answer begins with E and has 1 word.","fs-id1989759","f-4b211d7b727c"],["q10-b078a013f00a","s10.2-8e54897e809f","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsequence of events from motor neuron signaling to a skeletal muscle fiber to contraction of the fiber’s sarcomeres","excitation-contraction coupling",["motor end-plate","action potential","thick filament"],"excitation-contraction coupling: sequence of events from motor neuron signaling to a skeletal muscle fiber to contraction of the fiber’s sarcomeres.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","eip-362","f-135e24d8dcf8"],["q10-89c95b239f8d","s10.2-7e6e80035000","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nbundle of muscle fibers within a skeletal muscle","fascicle",["myofibril","actin","aponeurosis"],"fascicle: bundle of muscle fibers within a skeletal muscle.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id2278076","f-a03cbad4b8c9"],["q10-d37734f5cf9b","s10.2-298944a4ed79","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsarcolemma of muscle fiber at the neuromuscular junction, with receptors for the neurotransmitter acetylcholine","motor end-plate",["synaptic cleft","excitation-contraction coupling","smooth muscle"],"motor end-plate: sarcolemma of muscle fiber at the neuromuscular junction, with receptors for the neurotransmitter acetylcholine.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fig-ch10_02_04","f-cba858205acf"],["q10-ee312ca7c3a1","s10.2-ba3d572996d7","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmotor neuron and the group of muscle fibers it innervates","motor unit",["thick filament","excitation-contraction coupling","acetylcholine (ACh)"],"motor unit: motor neuron and the group of muscle fibers it innervates.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id1899987","f-f8efb2d2098d"],["q10-6b1024201e3e","s10.2-e663b56c33ae","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlong, cylindrical organelle that runs parallel within the muscle fiber and contains the sarcomeres","myofibril",["myosin","troponin","sarcoplasm"],"myofibril: long, cylindrical organelle that runs parallel within the muscle fiber and contains the sarcomeres.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id2044502","f-2323e2db5762"],["q10-a17e5ad6c04c","s10.2-90773d55b09f","10.2","The Neuromuscular Junction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsynapse between the axon terminal of a motor neuron and the section of the membrane of a muscle fiber with receptors for the acetylcholine released by the terminal","neuromuscular junction (NMJ)",["voltage-gated sodium channels","sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)","acetylcholine (ACh)"],"neuromuscular junction (NMJ): synapse between the axon terminal of a motor neuron and the section of the membrane of a muscle fiber with receptors for the acetylcholine released by the terminal.","The answer begins with N and has 3 words.","fs-id1854958","f-d167b219e348"],["q10-a6d2aa97752d","s10.2-9cfee6ebf073","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsignaling chemical released by nerve terminals that bind to and activate receptors on target cells","neurotransmitter",["perimysium","sarcomere","endomysium"],"neurotransmitter: signaling chemical released by nerve terminals that bind to and activate receptors on target cells.","The answer begins with N and has 1 word.","fs-id1899987","f-4acf2fe570bb"],["q10-0eecf8218780","s10.2-15d282c24901","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nconnective tissue that bundles skeletal muscle fibers into fascicles within a skeletal muscle","perimysium",["sarcoplasm","myofibril","triad"],"perimysium: connective tissue that bundles skeletal muscle fibers into fascicles within a skeletal muscle.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fig-ch10_02_01","f-210f1b6a9faf"],["q10-50a0bf01d095","s10.2-071626c5e7b8","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nplasma membrane of a skeletal muscle fiber","sarcolemma",["myosin","troponin","aponeurosis"],"sarcolemma: plasma membrane of a skeletal muscle fiber.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2125518","f-bb24dbe26b70"],["q10-cfb44e515b07","s10.2-d11b37f5b08d","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlongitudinally, repeating functional unit of skeletal muscle, with all of the contractile and associated proteins involved in contraction","sarcomere",["perimysium","troponin","sarcolemma"],"sarcomere: longitudinally, repeating functional unit of skeletal muscle, with all of the contractile and associated proteins involved in contraction.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2125518","f-c686ed554382"],["q10-eebbc08a0045","s10.2-25b3d6901347","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncytoplasm of a muscle cell","sarcoplasm",["T-tubule","myofibril","fascicle"],"sarcoplasm: cytoplasm of a muscle cell.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id2125518","f-f78c10f88049"],["q10-68cda3eb6be4","s10.2-4cb1c7579bc9","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nspecialized smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which stores, releases, and retrieves Ca ++","sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)",["voltage-gated sodium channels","neuromuscular junction (NMJ)","endomysium"],"sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR): specialized smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which stores, releases, and retrieves Ca ++.","The answer begins with S and has 3 words.","fs-id2125518","f-4d312f7b3b86"],["q10-1d45b9795574","s10.2-531bc9d8a833","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nspace between a nerve (axon) terminal and a motor end-plate","synaptic cleft",["thin filament","smooth muscle","skeletal muscle"],"synaptic cleft: space between a nerve (axon) terminal and a motor end-plate.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fig-ch10_02_04","f-2590352441be"],["q10-d575f97d348c","s10.2-e84eee0fefa5","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprojection of the sarcolemma into the interior of the cell","T-tubule",["aponeurosis","troponin","triad"],"T-tubule: projection of the sarcolemma into the interior of the cell.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1698692","f-60df204076b5"],["q10-2cb7873f16a4","s10.2-43de06c02b74","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthe thick myosin strands and their multiple heads projecting from the center of the sarcomere toward, but not all to way to, the Z-discs","thick filament",["smooth muscle","motor end-plate","skeletal muscle"],"thick filament: the thick myosin strands and their multiple heads projecting from the center of the sarcomere toward, but not all to way to, the Z-discs.","The answer begins with T and has 2 words.","fs-id2125518","f-7c3474640b67"],["q10-821a51a476e5","s10.2-8af8c0cff389","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthin strands of actin and its troponin-tropomyosin complex projecting from the Z-discs toward the center of the sarcomere","thin filament",["smooth muscle","motor end-plate","acetylcholine (ACh)"],"thin filament: thin strands of actin and its troponin-tropomyosin complex projecting from the Z-discs toward the center of the sarcomere.","The answer begins with T and has 2 words.","fs-id2125518","f-c8db1e5a2cc5"],["q10-e960b172e8ca","s10.2-67b0e6e4a7f8","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthe grouping of one T-tubule and two terminal cisternae","triad",["troponin","tropomyosin","fascicle"],"triad: the grouping of one T-tubule and two terminal cisternae.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1698692","f-7cd608d7d524"],["q10-751442df8460","s10.2-d0dd22eb6882","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmembrane proteins that open sodium channels in response to a sufficient voltage change, and initiate and transmit the action potential as Na + enters through the channel","voltage-gated sodium channels",["neuromuscular junction (NMJ)","sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)","sarcomere"],"voltage-gated sodium channels: membrane proteins that open sodium channels in response to a sufficient voltage change, and initiate and transmit the action potential as Na + enters through the channel.","The answer begins with V and has 3 words.","eip-536","f-3c4eb48618a0"],["q10-1a6bae8e9a67","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEach ____ is an organ that consists of various integrated tissues.","skeletal muscle",["motor unit","acetylcholine (ACh)","thin filament"],"Each skeletal muscle is an organ that consists of various integrated tissues.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989759","f-7dd65fc5763c"],["q10-544c5c05c249","s10.2-8fdacda63194","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ also separates muscle from other tissues and organs in the area, allowing the muscle to move independently.","epimysium",["neurotransmitter","myofibril","triad"],"The epimysium also separates muscle from other tissues and organs in the area, allowing the muscle to move independently.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989759","f-5ca6295d9079"],["q10-fce6eb64599c","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nInside each ____, muscle fibers are organized into individual bundles, each called a fascicle, by a middle layer of connective tissue called the perimysium.","skeletal muscle",["thin filament","motor unit","excitation-contraction coupling"],"Inside each skeletal muscle, muscle fibers are organized into individual bundles, each called a fascicle, by a middle layer of connective tissue called the perimysium.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2278076","f-60ab3e065be1"],["q10-2eb0c4f9338a","s10.2-f7c2d128ff2c","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nInside each fascicle, each muscle fiber is encased in a thin connective tissue layer of collagen and reticular fibers called the ____.","endomysium",["triad","sarcomere","sarcolemma"],"Inside each fascicle, each muscle fiber is encased in a thin connective tissue layer of collagen and reticular fibers called the endomysium.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2278076","f-7658170f84ff"],["q10-eed53e8765b6","s10.2-f7c2d128ff2c","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ contains the extracellular fluid and nutrients to support the muscle fiber.","endomysium",["myofibril","actin","troponin"],"The endomysium contains the extracellular fluid and nutrients to support the muscle fiber.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2278076","f-35c259b75bb3"],["q10-029e093daec9","s10.2-fcafb2752ed0","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn other places, the mysia may fuse with a broad, tendon-like sheet called an ____, or to fascia, the connective tissue between skin and bones.","aponeurosis",["neurotransmitter","triad","endomysium"],"In other places, the mysia may fuse with a broad, tendon-like sheet called an aponeurosis, or to fascia, the connective tissue between skin and bones.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2052461","f-de2386aac2ad"],["q10-e21a9c152be9","s10.2-fcafb2752ed0","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe broad sheet of connective tissue in the lower back that the latissimus dorsi muscles (the “lats”) fuse into is an example of an ____.","aponeurosis",["epimysium","endomysium","sarcolemma"],"The broad sheet of connective tissue in the lower back that the latissimus dorsi muscles (the “lats”) fuse into is an example of an aponeurosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2052461","f-5e82ebb68b88"],["q10-b6698cc39509","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEvery muscle fiber in a ____ is supplied by the axon branch of a somatic motor neuron, which signals the fiber to contract.","skeletal muscle",["thin filament","motor unit","motor end-plate"],"In addition, every muscle fiber in a skeletal muscle is supplied by the axon branch of a somatic motor neuron, which signals the fiber to contract.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1841712","f-36217ca3277c"],["q10-4c1bb5d91719","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nUnlike cardiac and smooth muscle, the only way to functionally contract a ____ is through signaling from the nervous system.","skeletal muscle",["excitation-contraction coupling","synaptic cleft","acetylcholine (ACh)"],"Unlike cardiac and smooth muscle, the only way to functionally contract a skeletal muscle is through signaling from the nervous system.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1841712","f-570619c46372"],["q10-358c6a2b7a15","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ fibers can be quite large for human cells, with diameters up to 100 μ m and lengths up to 30 cm (11.8 in) in the Sartorius of the upper leg.","skeletal muscle",["action potential","smooth muscle","thick filament"],"Skeletal muscle fibers can be quite large for human cells, with diameters up to 100 μ m and lengths up to 30 cm (11.8 in) in the Sartorius of the upper leg.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2122814","f-b197aebdf66b"],["q10-101c9aa92ebb","s10.2-4cb1c7579bc9","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSome other terminology associated with muscle fibers is rooted in the Greek sarco, which means “flesh.” The plasma membrane of muscle fibers is called the sarcolemma, the cytoplasm is referred to as sarcoplasm, and the specialized smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which stores, releases, and retrieves calcium ions (Ca ++ ) is called the ____.","sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR)",["voltage-gated sodium channels","neuromuscular junction (NMJ)","troponin"],"Some other terminology associated with muscle fibers is rooted in the Greek sarco, which means “flesh.” The plasma membrane of muscle fibers is called the sarcolemma, the cytoplasm is referred to as sarcoplasm, and the specialized smooth endoplasmic reticulum, which stores, releases, and retrieves calcium ions (Ca ++ ) is called the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2125518","f-d722c6917143"],["q10-3fd251d4f5dd","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs will soon be described, the functional unit of a ____ fiber is the sarcomere, a highly organized arrangement of the contractile myofilaments actin (thin filament) and myosin (thick filament), along with other support proteins.","skeletal muscle",["acetylcholine (ACh)","smooth muscle","motor unit"],"As will soon be described, the functional unit of a skeletal muscle fiber is the sarcomere, a highly organized arrangement of the contractile myofilaments actin (thin filament) and myosin (thick filament), along with other support proteins.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2125518","f-834c48aa2ff7"],["q10-16e49c444de5","s10.2-d11b37f5b08d","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ is the functional unit of the muscle fiber.","sarcomere",["myosin","myofibril","triad"],"The sarcomere is the functional unit of the muscle fiber.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2044502","f-8cad00b970e5"],["q10-2629d73f984f","s10.2-d11b37f5b08d","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEach ____ is approximately 2 μ m in length with a three-dimensional cylinder-like arrangement and is bordered by structures called Z-discs (also called Z-lines, because pictures are two-dimensional), to which the actin myofilaments are anchored.","sarcomere",["T-tubule","neurotransmitter","depolarize"],"Each sarcomere is approximately 2 μ m in length with a three-dimensional cylinder-like arrangement and is bordered by structures called Z-discs (also called Z-lines, because pictures are two-dimensional), to which the actin myofilaments are anchored.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2044502","f-374cf3015991"],["q10-e5f300c0fcbb","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEvery ____ fiber is supplied by a motor neuron at the NMJ.","skeletal muscle",["synaptic cleft","smooth muscle","excitation-contraction coupling"],"Every skeletal muscle fiber is supplied by a motor neuron at the NMJ.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1899987","f-5e14c9e486ba"],["q10-3f5685dbcd64","s10.2-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ is a special type of electrical signal that can travel along a cell membrane as a wave.","action potential",["acetylcholine (ACh)","thin filament","thick filament"],"An action potential is a special type of electrical signal that can travel along a cell membrane as a wave.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1421868","f-b6391c024373"],["q10-40a5e3a15220","s10.2-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe muscle fiber ____, which sweeps along the sarcolemma as a wave, is “coupled” to the actual contraction through the release of calcium ions (Ca ++ ) from the SR.","action potential",["excitation-contraction coupling","skeletal muscle","synaptic cleft"],"The muscle fiber action potential, which sweeps along the sarcolemma as a wave, is “coupled” to the actual contraction through the release of calcium ions (Ca ++ ) from the SR.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-362","f-dd1b952cc4a5"],["q10-d72c5b390a1e","s10.2-5479a81b0f86","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ then pulls the actin filaments toward the center, shortening the muscle fiber.","myosin",["perimysium","sarcolemma","endomysium"],"The myosin then pulls the actin filaments toward the center, shortening the muscle fiber.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-362","f-1efd2fbbec35"],["q10-2b4acdcdad36","s10.2-41d82176e2d1","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe motor neurons that tell the ____ fibers to contract originate in the spinal cord, with a smaller number located in the brainstem for activation of skeletal muscles of the face, head, and neck.","skeletal muscle",["excitation-contraction coupling","action potential","thin filament"],"The motor neurons that tell the skeletal muscle fibers to contract originate in the spinal cord, with a smaller number located in the brainstem for activation of skeletal muscles of the face, head, and neck.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2019473","f-a422afa201e0"],["q10-8d0da2f0e627","s10.2-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSignaling begins when a neuronal ____ travels along the axon of a motor neuron, and then along the individual branches to terminate at the NMJ.","action potential",["synaptic cleft","smooth muscle","thin filament"],"Signaling begins when a neuronal action potential travels along the axon of a motor neuron, and then along the individual branches to terminate at the NMJ.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2023666","f-5909f0e9a18c"],["q10-b87a1729e2d7","s10.2-646e27bbccea","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAt the NMJ, the axon terminal releases a chemical messenger, or neurotransmitter, called ____.","acetylcholine (ACh)",["action potential","smooth muscle","thick filament"],"At the NMJ, the axon terminal releases a chemical messenger, or neurotransmitter, called acetylcholine (ACh).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2023666","f-f932fef4a857"],["q10-1c5d7a30a7d1","s10.2-8e54897e809f","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSodium ions enter the muscle fiber, and an action potential rapidly spreads (or “fires”) along the entire membrane to initiate ____.","excitation-contraction coupling",["motor unit","synaptic cleft","motor end-plate"],"Sodium ions enter the muscle fiber, and an action potential rapidly spreads (or “fires”) along the entire membrane to initiate excitation-contraction coupling.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","eip-536","f-9a80dbef3b9f"],["q10-d96786daf21a","s10.2-8e54897e809f","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nPropagation of an action potential along the sarcolemma is the excitation portion of ____.","excitation-contraction coupling",["smooth muscle","skeletal muscle","thick filament"],"Propagation of an action potential along the sarcolemma is the excitation portion of excitation-contraction coupling.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1698692","f-de8d896194f3"],["q10-ce1d52602d37","s10.2-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nFor the ____ to reach the membrane of the SR, there are periodic invaginations in the sarcolemma, called T-tubules (“T” stands for “transverse”).","action potential",["thick filament","acetylcholine (ACh)","skeletal muscle"],"For the action potential to reach the membrane of the SR, there are periodic invaginations in the sarcolemma, called T-tubules (“T” stands for “transverse”).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1698692","f-9e92d21b773e"],["q10-ecd8f5222bf5","s10.2-e84eee0fefa5","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe arrangement of a ____ with the membranes of SR on either side is called a triad.","T-tubule",["depolarize","epimysium","sarcoplasm"],"The arrangement of a T-tubule with the membranes of SR on either side is called a triad.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1698692","f-17ebd5e35f06"],["q10-ad5600d07c33","s10.2-e663b56c33ae","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe triad surrounds the cylindrical structure called a ____, which contains actin and myosin.","myofibril",["neurotransmitter","depolarize","sarcolemma"],"The triad surrounds the cylindrical structure called a myofibril, which contains actin and myosin.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1698692","f-afb314404407"],["q10-de3bfb48a930","s10.2-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.2","Excitation-Contraction Coupling","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe T-tubules carry the ____ into the interior of the cell, which triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent SR, causing Ca ++ to diffuse out of the SR and into the sarcoplasm.","action potential",["motor end-plate","thin filament","synaptic cleft"],"The T-tubules carry the action potential into the interior of the cell, which triggers the opening of calcium channels in the membrane of the adjacent SR, causing Ca ++ to diffuse out of the SR and into the sarcoplasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1361245","f-5f36d193e97d"],["q10-339e165d2e3e","s10.3-f1d02837f944","10.3","Sources of ATP","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nproduction of ATP in the presence of oxygen","aerobic respiration",["power stroke","thick filament","pyruvic acid"],"aerobic respiration: production of ATP in the presence of oxygen.","The answer begins with A and has 2 words.","fs-id2072704","f-022490c16289"],["q10-df8d6969751e","s10.3-0e793af0b53c","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nenzyme that hydrolyzes ATP to ADP","ATPase",["actin","troponin","sarcolemma"],"ATPase: enzyme that hydrolyzes ATP to ADP.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1236880","f-ebcd31a11f40"],["q10-869d7ca9db47","s10.3-7ed58b35beae","10.3","Muscle Strength","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nloss of structural proteins from muscle fibers","atrophy",["myosin","depolarize","neurotransmitter"],"atrophy: loss of structural proteins from muscle fibers.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id1725236","f-79194a7744fc"],["q10-eae791ca2fb3","s10.3-633d97e5f326","10.3","Sources of ATP","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nphosphagen used to store energy from ATP and transfer it to muscle","creatine phosphate",["pyruvic acid","action potential","power stroke"],"creatine phosphate: phosphagen used to store energy from ATP and transfer it to muscle.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2072704","f-60ae11d41d65"],["q10-686ed5eec446","s10.3-eb4c57370d23","10.3","Sources of ATP","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nanaerobic breakdown of glucose to ATP","glycolysis",["myofibril","depolarize","atrophy"],"glycolysis: anaerobic breakdown of glucose to ATP.","The answer begins with G and has 1 word.","fs-id2072704","f-c277675d1fcb"],["q10-2b53beeb99fe","s10.3-1d3e6e07a6ce","10.3","Muscle Strength","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\naddition of structural proteins to muscle fibers","hypertrophy",["tropomyosin","glycolysis","myosin"],"hypertrophy: addition of structural proteins to muscle fibers.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id1725236","f-7cde8e4243fb"],["q10-2ca95f21d89d","s10.3-04872934d466","10.3","Sources of ATP","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nproduct of anaerobic glycolysis","lactic acid",["power stroke","aerobic respiration","pyruvic acid"],"lactic acid: product of anaerobic glycolysis.","The answer begins with L and has 2 words.","fig-ch10_03_05","f-3f65758c1567"],["q10-22fcede7ff29","s10.3-04779669e52e","10.3","Sources of ATP","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\namount of oxygen needed to compensate for ATP produced without oxygen during muscle contraction","oxygen debt",["skeletal muscle","aerobic respiration","action potential"],"oxygen debt: amount of oxygen needed to compensate for ATP produced without oxygen during muscle contraction.","The answer begins with O and has 2 words.","fs-id1841712","f-c93550274971"],["q10-7719c295c630","s10.3-ebae384edf98","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\naction of myosin pulling actin inward (toward the M line)","power stroke",["creatine phosphate","aerobic respiration","lactic acid"],"power stroke: action of myosin pulling actin inward (toward the M line).","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fig-ch10_03_04","f-b2308e40c8b7"],["q10-1e3bf50a11fc","s10.3-fe71467cf487","10.3","Sources of ATP","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nproduct of glycolysis that can be used in aerobic respiration or converted to lactic acid","pyruvic acid",["thick filament","action potential","power stroke"],"pyruvic acid: product of glycolysis that can be used in aerobic respiration or converted to lactic acid.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fig-ch10_03_05","f-a86930a923c6"],["q10-4713eac79c6e","s10.3-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.3","Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe local membrane of the fiber will depolarize as positively charged sodium ions (Na + ) enter, triggering an ____ that spreads to the rest of the membrane which will depolarize, including the T-tubules.","action potential",["skeletal muscle","lactic acid","thin filament"],"The local membrane of the fiber will depolarize as positively charged sodium ions (Na + ) enter, triggering an action potential that spreads to the rest of the membrane which will depolarize, including the T-tubules.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707608","f-56edd3f5c90d"],["q10-a6acbd8ccba4","s10.3-25b3d6901347","10.3","Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs long as Ca ++ ions remain in the ____ to bind to troponin, which keeps the actin-binding sites “unshielded,” and as long as ATP is available to drive the cross-bridge cycling and the pulling of actin strands by myosin, the muscle fiber will continue to shorten to an anatomical limit.","sarcoplasm",["myofibril","depolarize","ATPase"],"As long as Ca ++ ions remain in the sarcoplasm to bind to troponin, which keeps the actin-binding sites “unshielded,” and as long as ATP is available to drive the cross-bridge cycling and the pulling of actin strands by myosin, the muscle fiber will continue to shorten to an anatomical limit.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1707608","f-c66ea370dda0"],["q10-5d040df4a502","s10.3-071626c5e7b8","10.3","Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMuscle contraction usually stops when signaling from the motor neuron ends, which repolarizes the ____ and T-tubules, and closes the voltage-gated calcium channels in the SR.","sarcolemma",["glycolysis","sarcomere","hypertrophy"],"Muscle contraction usually stops when signaling from the motor neuron ends, which repolarizes the sarcolemma and T-tubules, and closes the voltage-gated calcium channels in the SR.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2023667","f-0f221e8512d6"],["q10-07173c1dcd4e","s10.3-621e2b6031eb","10.3","Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCa ++ ions are then pumped back into the SR, which causes the ____ to reshield (or re-cover) the binding sites on the actin strands.","tropomyosin",["atrophy","sarcolemma","sarcomere"],"Ca ++ ions are then pumped back into the SR, which causes the tropomyosin to reshield (or re-cover) the binding sites on the actin strands.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2023667","f-ecf1f831faf0"],["q10-be0446626c62","s10.3-5479a81b0f86","10.3","Muscle Fiber Contraction and Relaxation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe contraction of a striated muscle fiber occurs as the sarcomeres, linearly arranged within myofibrils, shorten as ____ heads pull on the actin filaments.","myosin",["atrophy","myofibril","sarcomere"],"The contraction of a striated muscle fiber occurs as the sarcomeres, linearly arranged within myofibrils, shorten as myosin heads pull on the actin filaments.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1632981","f-46d84e97f092"],["q10-af074b3aa746","s10.3-25b3d6901347","10.3","The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe sliding can only occur when myosin-binding sites on the actin filaments are exposed by a series of steps that begins with Ca ++ entry into the ____.","sarcoplasm",["neurotransmitter","ATPase","tropomyosin"],"The sliding can only occur when myosin-binding sites on the actin filaments are exposed by a series of steps that begins with Ca ++ entry into the sarcoplasm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1478868","f-2a9402ce61d0"],["q10-39f2e18176d6","s10.3-621e2b6031eb","10.3","The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is a protein that winds around the chains of the actin filament and covers the myosin-binding sites to prevent actin from binding to myosin.","Tropomyosin",["atrophy","sarcolemma","sarcomere"],"Tropomyosin is a protein that winds around the chains of the actin filament and covers the myosin-binding sites to prevent actin from binding to myosin.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1645190","f-85509afcea79"],["q10-10fc8dc2fd99","s10.3-621e2b6031eb","10.3","The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe troponin-____ complex prevents the myosin “heads” from binding to the active sites on the actin microfilaments.","tropomyosin",["glycolysis","ATPase","atrophy"],"The troponin-tropomyosin complex prevents the myosin “heads” from binding to the active sites on the actin microfilaments.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1645190","f-443581cc9bae"],["q10-045bff79e541","s10.3-621e2b6031eb","10.3","The Sliding Filament Model of Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTo initiate muscle contraction, ____ has to expose the myosin-binding site on an actin filament to allow cross-bridge formation between the actin and myosin microfilaments.","tropomyosin",["depolarize","glycolysis","troponin"],"To initiate muscle contraction, tropomyosin has to expose the myosin-binding site on an actin filament to allow cross-bridge formation between the actin and myosin microfilaments.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2292610","f-02ecc6c13f7e"],["q10-5b2f60b1528a","s10.3-5479a81b0f86","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEach cycle requires energy, and the action of the ____ heads in the sarcomeres repetitively pulling on the thin filaments also requires energy, which is provided by ATP.","myosin",["myofibril","sarcoplasm","ATPase"],"Each cycle requires energy, and the action of the myosin heads in the sarcomeres repetitively pulling on the thin filaments also requires energy, which is provided by ATP.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2030918","f-efd3560bdb80"],["q10-9f7990ffaa34","s10.3-0de11d6c6b9b","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs ____ is pulled, the filaments move approximately 10 nm toward the M-line.","actin",["myosin","glycolysis","ATPase"],"As actin is pulled, the filaments move approximately 10 nm toward the M-line.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1471902","f-4f06932a379c"],["q10-675bc1ddbf1f","s10.3-5479a81b0f86","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn the absence of ATP, the ____ head will not detach from actin.","myosin",["depolarize","hypertrophy","neurotransmitter"],"In the absence of ATP, the myosin head will not detach from actin.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1471902","f-b4fe0b34e6d5"],["q10-15bdf2dbf6aa","s10.3-5479a81b0f86","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the ____ head into a cocked position ( e ).","myosin",["depolarize","glycolysis","myofibril"],"The energy released during ATP hydrolysis changes the angle of the myosin head into a cocked position ( e ).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1236880","f-8edb2be8852d"],["q10-3b87352521a7","s10.3-5479a81b0f86","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ head is now in position for further movement.","myosin",["hypertrophy","ATPase","glycolysis"],"The myosin head is now in position for further movement.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1236880","f-ed520b9cdcda"],["q10-b249494b4547","s10.3-43de06c02b74","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNote that each ____ of roughly 300 myosin molecules has multiple myosin heads, and many cross-bridges form and break continuously during muscle contraction.","thick filament",["skeletal muscle","power stroke","lactic acid"],"Note that each thick filament of roughly 300 myosin molecules has multiple myosin heads, and many cross-bridges form and break continuously during muscle contraction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2059667","f-2f6525258cce"],["q10-3e8ad15d1f68","s10.3-5479a81b0f86","10.3","ATP and Muscle Contraction","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWith no further ATP production possible, there is no ATP available for ____ heads to detach from the actin-binding sites, so the cross-bridges stay in place, causing the rigidity in the skeletal muscles.","myosin",["sarcolemma","troponin","neurotransmitter"],"With no further ATP production possible, there is no ATP available for myosin heads to detach from the actin-binding sites, so the cross-bridges stay in place, causing the rigidity in the skeletal muscles.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2059667","f-3a034bf2cd72"],["q10-d960197d5d36","s10.3-f1d02837f944","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere are three mechanisms by which ATP can be regenerated in muscle cells: creatine phosphate metabolism, anaerobic glycolysis, and ____.","aerobic respiration",["oxygen debt","thick filament","pyruvic acid"],"There are three mechanisms by which ATP can be regenerated in muscle cells: creatine phosphate metabolism, anaerobic glycolysis, and aerobic respiration.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2072704","f-5bf9a0f45572"],["q10-6e15edae2155","s10.3-633d97e5f326","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs the ATP produced by ____ is depleted, muscles turn to glycolysis as an ATP source.","creatine phosphate",["pyruvic acid","aerobic respiration","thin filament"],"As the ATP produced by creatine phosphate is depleted, muscles turn to glycolysis as an ATP source.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2142755","f-c1b080470107"],["q10-1b511e9b7b5e","s10.3-eb4c57370d23","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThus, the switch to ____ results in a slower rate of ATP availability to the muscle.","glycolysis",["actin","ATPase","hypertrophy"],"Thus, the switch to glycolysis results in a slower rate of ATP availability to the muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2142755","f-3fee95640fde"],["q10-ccb013e135fc","s10.3-eb4c57370d23","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe sugar used in ____ can be provided by blood glucose or by metabolizing glycogen that is stored in the muscle.","glycolysis",["sarcolemma","sarcoplasm","actin"],"The sugar used in glycolysis can be provided by blood glucose or by metabolizing glycogen that is stored in the muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2142755","f-79f2b2aede95"],["q10-2b09208e3582","s10.3-f1d02837f944","10.3","Sources of ATP","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf oxygen is available, pyruvic acid is used in ____.","aerobic respiration",["action potential","power stroke","thin filament"],"If oxygen is available, pyruvic acid is used in aerobic respiration.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2346865","f-d15a372ae1f6"],["q10-3d14019dcaa7","s10.3-fe71467cf487","10.3","Sources of ATP","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf oxygen is not available, ____ is converted to lactic acid, which may contribute to muscle fatigue.","pyruvic acid",["power stroke","skeletal muscle","thin filament"],"However, if oxygen is not available, pyruvic acid is converted to lactic acid, which may contribute to muscle fatigue.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2346865","f-5c60555163da"],["q10-74e49797639c","s10.3-f1d02837f944","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is the breakdown of glucose or other nutrients in the presence of oxygen (O 2 ) to produce carbon dioxide, water, and ATP.","Aerobic respiration",["oxygen debt","thin filament","action potential"],"Aerobic respiration is the breakdown of glucose or other nutrients in the presence of oxygen (O₂ ) to produce carbon dioxide, water, and ATP.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1909043","f-3ae22e5c03f0"],["q10-8e227207f252","s10.3-f1d02837f944","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe inputs for ____ include glucose circulating in the bloodstream, pyruvic acid, and fatty acids.","aerobic respiration",["thin filament","power stroke","skeletal muscle"],"The inputs for aerobic respiration include glucose circulating in the bloodstream, pyruvic acid, and fatty acids.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1909043","f-ceabba40bbe0"],["q10-4b4ed4ddf471","s10.3-f1d02837f944","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is much more efficient than anaerobic glycolysis, producing approximately 36 ATPs per molecule of glucose versus four from glycolysis.","Aerobic respiration",["action potential","oxygen debt","power stroke"],"Aerobic respiration is much more efficient than anaerobic glycolysis, producing approximately 36 ATPs per molecule of glucose versus four from glycolysis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1909043","f-a8bba201afed"],["q10-5cdd68c370d9","s10.3-04872934d466","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ buildup may lower intracellular pH, affecting enzyme and protein activity.","lactic acid",["aerobic respiration","action potential","oxygen debt"],"Lactic acid buildup may lower intracellular pH, affecting enzyme and protein activity.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2302886","f-bebe0e653c35"],["q10-8a0c7c3c713f","s10.3-071626c5e7b8","10.3","Sources of ATP","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLong periods of sustained exercise may damage the SR and the ____, resulting in impaired Ca ++ regulation.","sarcolemma",["tropomyosin","troponin","sarcoplasm"],"Long periods of sustained exercise may damage the SR and the sarcolemma, resulting in impaired Ca ++ regulation.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2302886","f-570da0f5f5f2"],["q10-e53c84ff63e6","s10.3-04779669e52e","10.3","Sources of ATP","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIntense muscle activity results in an ____, which is the amount of oxygen needed to compensate for ATP produced without oxygen during muscle contraction.","oxygen debt",["lactic acid","thick filament","aerobic respiration"],"Intense muscle activity results in an oxygen debt, which is the amount of oxygen needed to compensate for ATP produced without oxygen during muscle contraction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1841712","f-52e34ad48458"],["q10-35d7686cda80","s10.3-633d97e5f326","10.3","Sources of ATP","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nOxygen is required to restore ATP and ____ levels, convert lactic acid to pyruvic acid, and, in the liver, to convert lactic acid into glucose or glycogen.","creatine phosphate",["skeletal muscle","thick filament","oxygen debt"],"Oxygen is required to restore ATP and creatine phosphate levels, convert lactic acid to pyruvic acid, and, in the liver, to convert lactic acid into glucose or glycogen.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1841712","f-140eb7009a05"],["q10-40863116a605","s10.3-04779669e52e","10.3","Sources of ATP","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nUntil the ____ has been met, oxygen intake is elevated, even after exercise has stopped.","oxygen debt",["skeletal muscle","thin filament","lactic acid"],"Until the oxygen debt has been met, oxygen intake is elevated, even after exercise has stopped.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1841712","f-665e492df731"],["q10-00c07260e9c0","s10.3-25b3d6901347","10.3","Relaxation of a Skeletal Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nATP-driven pumps will move Ca ++ out of the ____ back into the SR.","sarcoplasm",["actin","atrophy","myosin"],"ATP-driven pumps will move Ca ++ out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1639118","f-b483dfd014a7"],["q10-e58a7a9ec743","s10.3-41d82176e2d1","10.3","Muscle Strength","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe number of ____ fibers in a given muscle is genetically determined and does not change.","skeletal muscle",["thin filament","action potential","thick filament"],"The number of skeletal muscle fibers in a given muscle is genetically determined and does not change.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1725236","f-09fa62977a13"],["q10-8020db6aa106","s10.3-41d82176e2d1","10.3","Muscle Strength","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDecreased use of a ____ results in atrophy, where the number of sarcomeres and myofibrils disappear (but not the number of muscle fibers).","skeletal muscle",["pyruvic acid","action potential","creatine phosphate"],"Likewise, decreased use of a skeletal muscle results in atrophy, where the number of sarcomeres and myofibrils disappear (but not the number of muscle fibers).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1725236","f-fb8777d6112d"],["q10-64891e2302ed","s10.3-071626c5e7b8","10.3","Muscular System","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWithout sufficient dystrophin, muscle contractions cause the ____ to tear, causing an influx of Ca ++, leading to cellular damage and muscle fiber degradation.","sarcolemma",["tropomyosin","actin","myosin"],"Without sufficient dystrophin, muscle contractions cause the sarcolemma to tear, causing an influx of Ca ++, leading to cellular damage and muscle fiber degradation.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1932393","f-0066c372a486"],["q10-54cfbae1a959","s10.4-ba0d2299dd75","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscle contraction that shortens the muscle to move a load","concentric contraction",["muscle tension","wave summation","eccentric contraction"],"concentric contraction: muscle contraction that shortens the muscle to move a load.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id2110034","f-bf86fcb97885"],["q10-50ed1f312581","s10.4-def7b1c2a051","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ntwitch contraction phase when tension increases","contraction phase",["eccentric contraction","skeletal muscle","wave summation"],"contraction phase: twitch contraction phase when tension increases.","The answer begins with C and has 2 words.","fs-id1989390","f-2309a30c512d"],["q10-214d94b7afb9","s10.4-945ec2c8f7ee","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscle contraction that lengthens the muscle as the tension is diminished","eccentric contraction",["wave summation","contraction phase","relaxation phase"],"eccentric contraction: muscle contraction that lengthens the muscle as the tension is diminished.","The answer begins with E and has 2 words.","fs-id2110034","f-7228f0a79e0c"],["q10-3c8e5976c00d","s10.4-022c8c8bbdd5","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmodification of contraction strength","graded muscle response",["skeletal muscle","motor unit","myosin"],"graded muscle response: modification of contraction strength.","The answer begins with G and has 3 words.","fs-id2270684","f-d4fd8ef4eb00"],["q10-1de1e43e0d42","s10.4-534c81925f40","10.4","Muscle Tone","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nabnormally high muscle tone","hypertonia",["actin","hypotonia","sarcomere"],"hypertonia: abnormally high muscle tone.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id2268838","f-8d2ecf2627ff"],["q10-2786ab54099d","s10.4-772029ea3ee4","10.4","Muscle Tone","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nabnormally low muscle tone caused by the absence of low-level contractions","hypotonia",["tropomyosin","actin","recruitment"],"hypotonia: abnormally low muscle tone caused by the absence of low-level contractions.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id2268838","f-c55cd1a65143"],["q10-fa5e3ff1f85b","s10.4-c3f1c92a328d","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscle contraction that occurs with no change in muscle length","isometric contraction",["contraction phase","wave summation","relaxation phase"],"isometric contraction: muscle contraction that occurs with no change in muscle length.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id2203482","f-de3372d353d0"],["q10-936824e9b60b","s10.4-e50f1164862f","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nthe time when a twitch does not produce contraction","latent period",["skeletal muscle","contraction phase","action potential"],"latent period: the time when a twitch does not produce contraction.","The answer begins with L and has 2 words.","fs-id1989390","f-346e31ddff66"],["q10-c9083a7165e9","s10.4-0eeb2861f340","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nlow levels of muscle contraction that occur when a muscle is not producing movement","muscle tone",["action potential","skeletal muscle","excitation-contraction coupling"],"muscle tone: low levels of muscle contraction that occur when a muscle is not producing movement.","The answer begins with M and has 2 words.","fs-id2103513","f-0b207dd1bca0"],["q10-96a434fbad98","s10.4-77ea46846afe","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ninstrument used to measure twitch tension","myogram",["hypertonia","hypotonia","sarcomere"],"myogram: instrument used to measure twitch tension.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1989390","f-00aad7d3e439"],["q10-572a7801400c","s10.4-fedb98f755b6","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nincrease in the number of motor units involved in contraction","recruitment",["hypertonia","treppe","myosin"],"recruitment: increase in the number of motor units involved in contraction.","The answer begins with R and has 1 word.","fs-id2103513","f-cecb3c57694c"],["q10-16d1bfcba4a7","s10.4-604b4822a770","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nperiod after twitch contraction when tension decreases","relaxation phase",["excitation-contraction coupling","eccentric contraction","skeletal muscle"],"relaxation phase: period after twitch contraction when tension decreases.","The answer begins with R and has 2 words.","fs-id1989390","f-32725bfdc4e3"],["q10-9da8a8bfe197","s10.4-7352f8bb58ba","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\na continuous fused contraction","tetanus",["hypertonia","actin","sarcolemma"],"tetanus: a continuous fused contraction.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fig-ch10_04_04","f-9ef05d714e14"],["q10-ede6f8588ba2","s10.4-392ef4627563","10.4","Treppe","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstepwise increase in contraction tension","treppe",["sarcomere","myogram","hypotonia"],"treppe: stepwise increase in contraction tension.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1217311","f-f2a97b639ee8"],["q10-21e766c4882a","s10.4-095ee8f5f442","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsingle contraction produced by one action potential","twitch",["treppe","hypertonia","hypotonia"],"twitch: single contraction produced by one action potential.","The answer begins with T and has 1 word.","fs-id1989390","f-7897dcf2e7f6"],["q10-ef1eebbdcf33","s10.4-555b8e70189f","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\naddition of successive neural stimuli to produce greater contraction","wave summation",["latent period","relaxation phase","skeletal muscle"],"wave summation: addition of successive neural stimuli to produce greater contraction.","The answer begins with W and has 2 words.","fs-id2017671","f-ec3112d227e3"],["q10-6970f1a495b6","s10.4-41d82176e2d1","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nTo move an object, referred to as load, the sarcomeres in the muscle fibers of the ____ must shorten.","skeletal muscle",["contraction phase","action potential","concentric contraction"],"To move an object, referred to as load, the sarcomeres in the muscle fibers of the skeletal muscle must shorten.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2059686","f-2f95254738c7"],["q10-b1c432d6e0d5","s10.4-ac8bdc808a8f","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe force generated by the contraction of the muscle (or shortening of the sarcomeres) is called ____.","muscle tension",["latent period","relaxation phase","wave summation"],"The force generated by the contraction of the muscle (or shortening of the sarcomeres) is called muscle tension.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2059686","f-79b3e9a86e3f"],["q10-c47f8d1ba877","s10.4-d84a972b1e55","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMuscle tension also is generated when the muscle is contracting against a load that does not move, resulting in two main types of skeletal muscle contractions: ____ and isometric contractions.","isotonic contractions",["wave summation","excitation-contraction coupling","latent period"],"However, muscle tension also is generated when the muscle is contracting against a load that does not move, resulting in two main types of skeletal muscle contractions: isotonic contractions and isometric contractions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2059686","f-9cfeabb3275a"],["q10-9527b9f8a93a","s10.4-d84a972b1e55","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn ____, where the tension in the muscle stays constant, a load is moved as the length of the muscle changes (shortens).","isotonic contractions",["muscle tone","latent period","wave summation"],"In isotonic contractions, where the tension in the muscle stays constant, a load is moved as the length of the muscle changes (shortens).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110034","f-d1570201a2ef"],["q10-0d2a61721880","s10.4-d84a972b1e55","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere are two types of ____: concentric and eccentric.","isotonic contractions",["latent period","skeletal muscle","motor unit"],"There are two types of isotonic contractions: concentric and eccentric.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110034","f-93773fb57640"],["q10-0907388a4fa4","s10.4-ba0d2299dd75","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ involves the muscle shortening to move a load.","concentric contraction",["isometric contraction","eccentric contraction","muscle tone"],"A concentric contraction involves the muscle shortening to move a load.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110034","f-7bc07f6ed7fc"],["q10-8b3decb9cd56","s10.4-945ec2c8f7ee","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ occurs as the muscle tension diminishes and the muscle lengthens.","eccentric contraction",["motor unit","muscle tone","isometric contraction"],"An eccentric contraction occurs as the muscle tension diminishes and the muscle lengthens.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2110034","f-31b2d44926de"],["q10-8ec193c3ca32","s10.4-c3f1c92a328d","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ occurs as the muscle produces tension without changing the angle of a skeletal joint.","isometric contraction",["excitation-contraction coupling","eccentric contraction","muscle tension"],"An isometric contraction occurs as the muscle produces tension without changing the angle of a skeletal joint.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2203482","f-762ac2fec36e"],["q10-72d21847ef1c","s10.4-ac8bdc808a8f","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIsometric contractions involve sarcomere shortening and increasing ____, but do not move a load, as the force produced cannot overcome the resistance provided by the load.","muscle tension",["action potential","contraction phase","latent period"],"Isometric contractions involve sarcomere shortening and increasing muscle tension, but do not move a load, as the force produced cannot overcome the resistance provided by the load.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2203482","f-18ad311a99b9"],["q10-7469f3844c8c","s10.4-ac8bdc808a8f","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf one attempts to lift a hand weight that is too heavy, there will be sarcomere activation and shortening to a point, and ever-increasing ____, but no change in the angle of the elbow joint.","muscle tension",["wave summation","excitation-contraction coupling","isometric contraction"],"For example, if one attempts to lift a hand weight that is too heavy, there will be sarcomere activation and shortening to a point, and ever-increasing muscle tension, but no change in the angle of the elbow joint.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2203482","f-936f88874f0e"],["q10-78cf4cd1ba85","s10.4-0eeb2861f340","10.4","Nervous System Control of Muscle Tension","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nNeural control regulates concentric, eccentric and isometric contractions, muscle fiber recruitment, and ____.","muscle tone",["wave summation","latent period","contraction phase"],"Neural control regulates concentric, eccentric and isometric contractions, muscle fiber recruitment, and muscle tone.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2103513","f-8c48e015d9fc"],["q10-2f918d8e1958","s10.4-41d82176e2d1","10.4","Motor Units","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAs you have learned, every ____ fiber must be innervated by the axon terminal of a motor neuron in order to contract.","skeletal muscle",["wave summation","concentric contraction","muscle tone"],"As you have learned, every skeletal muscle fiber must be innervated by the axon terminal of a motor neuron in order to contract.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2131675","f-357b4488fb37"],["q10-5a8e68ab05f1","s10.4-ba3d572996d7","10.4","Motor Units","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe actual group of muscle fibers in a muscle innervated by a single motor neuron is called a ____.","motor unit",["relaxation phase","excitation-contraction coupling","action potential"],"The actual group of muscle fibers in a muscle innervated by a single motor neuron is called a motor unit.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2131675","f-7eda71614326"],["q10-4276169de70f","s10.4-ba3d572996d7","10.4","Motor Units","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe size of a ____ is variable depending on the nature of the muscle.","motor unit",["muscle tension","muscle tone","contraction phase"],"The size of a motor unit is variable depending on the nature of the muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2131675","f-b695d3944c5a"],["q10-570ee5bb9739","s10.4-ba3d572996d7","10.4","Motor Units","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA small ____ is an arrangement where a single motor neuron supplies a small number of muscle fibers in a muscle.","motor unit",["muscle tone","contraction phase","isometric contraction"],"A small motor unit is an arrangement where a single motor neuron supplies a small number of muscle fibers in a muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1861281","f-9fdabea3da5d"],["q10-e5ee2ba9f170","s10.4-ba3d572996d7","10.4","Motor Units","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA large ____ is an arrangement where a single motor neuron supplies a large number of muscle fibers in a muscle.","motor unit",["relaxation phase","concentric contraction","muscle tone"],"A large motor unit is an arrangement where a single motor neuron supplies a large number of muscle fibers in a muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2228947","f-4b0baa40d346"],["q10-94f5e9ce900c","s10.4-41d82176e2d1","10.4","Motor Units","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe nervous system uses recruitment as a mechanism to efficiently utilize a ____.","skeletal muscle",["action potential","isometric contraction","motor unit"],"The nervous system uses recruitment as a mechanism to efficiently utilize a skeletal muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1968479","f-9efc2e959d23"],["q10-3a0f8af524f8","s10.4-d11b37f5b08d","10.4","The Length-Tension Range of a Sarcomere","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf a ____ is shortened beyond 80 percent, the zone of overlap is reduced with the thin filaments jutting beyond the last of the myosin heads and shrinks the H zone, which is normally composed of myosin tails.","sarcomere",["tetanus","treppe","sarcolemma"],"If a sarcomere is shortened beyond 80 percent, the zone of overlap is reduced with the thin filaments jutting beyond the last of the myosin heads and shrinks the H zone, which is normally composed of myosin tails.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2252591","f-286851a805f0"],["q10-743aab25cd0a","s10.4-d11b37f5b08d","10.4","The Length-Tension Range of a Sarcomere","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf the muscle is stretched to the point where thick and thin filaments do not overlap at all, no cross-bridges can be formed, and no tension is produced in that ____.","sarcomere",["sarcoplasm","hypotonia","sarcolemma"],"If the muscle is stretched to the point where thick and thin filaments do not overlap at all, no cross-bridges can be formed, and no tension is produced in that sarcomere.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2252591","f-70cbb4421852"],["q10-8b9e87a1f085","s10.4-095ee8f5f442","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ can last for a few milliseconds or 100 milliseconds, depending on the muscle type.","twitch",["tetanus","hypotonia","tropomyosin"],"A twitch can last for a few milliseconds or 100 milliseconds, depending on the muscle type.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989390","f-008f18cc3ae1"],["q10-55b0ff348e28","s10.4-77ea46846afe","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe tension produced by a single twitch can be measured by a ____, an instrument that measures the amount of tension produced over time.","myogram",["recruitment","tetanus","tropomyosin"],"The tension produced by a single twitch can be measured by a myogram, an instrument that measures the amount of tension produced over time.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989390","f-8df06b56bdb0"],["q10-f7dbf1a60269","s10.4-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe first phase is the latent period, during which the ____ is being propagated along the sarcolemma and Ca ++ ions are released from the SR.","action potential",["motor unit","muscle tension","eccentric contraction"],"The first phase is the latent period, during which the action potential is being propagated along the sarcolemma and Ca ++ ions are released from the SR.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989390","f-1e35801c20d5"],["q10-1ef5d9d4ba3e","s10.4-621e2b6031eb","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe Ca ++ ions in the sarcoplasm have bound to troponin, ____ has shifted away from myosin-binding sites on actin, cross-bridges have formed, and sarcomeres are actively shortening to the point of peak tension.","tropomyosin",["twitch","sarcomere","hypotonia"],"The Ca ++ ions in the sarcoplasm have bound to troponin, tropomyosin has shifted away from myosin-binding sites on actin, cross-bridges have formed, and sarcomeres are actively shortening to the point of peak tension.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989390","f-b4935ef8a01c"],["q10-28ee1f44495b","s10.4-604b4822a770","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe last phase is the ____, when tension decreases as contraction stops.","relaxation phase",["muscle tension","wave summation","action potential"],"The last phase is the relaxation phase, when tension decreases as contraction stops.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1989390","f-ed938298523f"],["q10-0342a472274a","s10.4-41d82176e2d1","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe frequency of action potentials (nerve impulses) from a motor neuron and the number of motor neurons transmitting action potentials both affect the tension produced in ____.","skeletal muscle",["contraction phase","wave summation","isometric contraction"],"The frequency of action potentials (nerve impulses) from a motor neuron and the number of motor neurons transmitting action potentials both affect the tension produced in skeletal muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2270684","f-0e83be1fa99f"],["q10-285607fe2c4e","s10.4-41d82176e2d1","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe rate at which a motor neuron fires action potentials affects the tension produced in the ____.","skeletal muscle",["muscle tone","concentric contraction","muscle tension"],"The rate at which a motor neuron fires action potentials affects the tension produced in the skeletal muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2017671","f-d5a45aef4441"],["q10-c312cc065491","s10.4-ba3d572996d7","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSummation results in greater contraction of the ____.","motor unit",["excitation-contraction coupling","action potential","relaxation phase"],"Summation results in greater contraction of the motor unit.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2017671","f-2fddce40b08f"],["q10-b5538a041b87","s10.4-604b4822a770","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDuring incomplete tetanus, the muscle goes through quick cycles of contraction with a short ____ for each.","relaxation phase",["excitation-contraction coupling","motor unit","wave summation"],"During incomplete tetanus, the muscle goes through quick cycles of contraction with a short relaxation phase for each.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1917008","f-71286bd8098c"],["q10-5f1efc5fc24a","s10.4-25b3d6901347","10.4","The Frequency of Motor Neuron Stimulation","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDuring tetanus, the concentration of Ca ++ ions in the ____ allows virtually all of the sarcomeres to form cross-bridges and shorten, so that a contraction can continue uninterrupted (until the muscle fatigues and can no longer produce tension).","sarcoplasm",["recruitment","actin","tropomyosin"],"During tetanus, the concentration of Ca ++ ions in the sarcoplasm allows virtually all of the sarcomeres to form cross-bridges and shorten, so that a contraction can continue uninterrupted (until the muscle fatigues and can no longer produce tension).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1984736","f-bef6361e96e1"],["q10-d49566dd02fc","s10.4-41d82176e2d1","10.4","Treppe","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nWhen a ____ has been dormant for an extended period and then activated to contract, with all other things being equal, the initial contractions generate about one-half the force of later contractions.","skeletal muscle",["latent period","wave summation","muscle tension"],"When a skeletal muscle has been dormant for an extended period and then activated to contract, with all other things being equal, the initial contractions generate about one-half the force of later contractions.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1217311","f-7804627d2986"],["q10-6d16b97ccc22","s10.4-ac8bdc808a8f","10.4","Treppe","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe ____ increases in a graded manner that to some looks like a set of stairs.","muscle tension",["action potential","skeletal muscle","wave summation"],"The muscle tension increases in a graded manner that to some looks like a set of stairs.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1217311","f-4b3b6971f484"],["q10-3c1475041ecf","s10.4-0eeb2861f340","10.4","Muscle Tone","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe tension produced by ____ allows muscles to continually stabilize joints and maintain posture.","muscle tone",["contraction phase","motor unit","skeletal muscle"],"The tension produced by muscle tone allows muscles to continually stabilize joints and maintain posture.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2341110","f-41c83e6136e6"],["q10-5348b7af57c1","s10.4-0eeb2861f340","10.4","Muscle Tone","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ is accomplished by a complex interaction between the nervous system and skeletal muscles that results in the activation of a few motor units at a time, most likely in a cyclical manner.","Muscle tone",["relaxation phase","motor unit","contraction phase"],"Muscle tone is accomplished by a complex interaction between the nervous system and skeletal muscles that results in the activation of a few motor units at a time, most likely in a cyclical manner.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2350441","f-58e3b587b557"],["q10-13b904309e07","s10.4-0eeb2861f340","10.4","Muscle Tone","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nConversely, excessive ____ is referred to as hypertonia, accompanied by hyperreflexia (excessive reflex responses), often the result of damage to upper motor neurons in the CNS.","muscle tone",["concentric contraction","action potential","motor unit"],"Conversely, excessive muscle tone is referred to as hypertonia, accompanied by hyperreflexia (excessive reflex responses), often the result of damage to upper motor neurons in the CNS.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2268838","f-a7c879e574f7"],["q10-5b1522c1e165","s10.4-0eeb2861f340","10.4","Muscle Tone","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHypertonia can present with muscle rigidity (as seen in Parkinson’s disease) or spasticity, a phasic change in ____, where a limb will “snap” back from passive stretching (as seen in some strokes).","muscle tone",["contraction phase","latent period","isometric contraction"],"Hypertonia can present with muscle rigidity (as seen in Parkinson’s disease) or spasticity, a phasic change in muscle tone, where a limb will “snap” back from passive stretching (as seen in some strokes).","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2268838","f-6448e1e38e83"],["q10-22b32771e539","s10.5-a583448c9459","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscle fiber that primarily uses anaerobic glycolysis","fast glycolytic (FG)",["slow oxidative (SO)","fast oxidative (FO)","skeletal muscle"],"fast glycolytic (FG): muscle fiber that primarily uses anaerobic glycolysis.","The answer begins with F and has 3 words.","fs-id1282811","f-428ef3c7793c"],["q10-f4dfdccda5cb","s10.5-c7b91d5600a2","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nintermediate muscle fiber that is between slow oxidative and fast glycolytic fibers","fast oxidative (FO)",["slow oxidative (SO)","fast glycolytic (FG)","aerobic respiration"],"fast oxidative (FO): intermediate muscle fiber that is between slow oxidative and fast glycolytic fibers.","The answer begins with F and has 3 words.","fs-id1282811","f-3c74a36f4ab7"],["q10-6f582995076b","s10.5-761ad0303a30","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscle fiber that primarily uses aerobic respiration","slow oxidative (SO)",["fast oxidative (FO)","fast glycolytic (FG)","skeletal muscle"],"slow oxidative (SO): muscle fiber that primarily uses aerobic respiration.","The answer begins with S and has 3 words.","fs-id1282811","f-153b0986f937"],["q10-a8a7bd077ae4","s10.5-761ad0303a30","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ fibers contract relatively slowly and use aerobic respiration (oxygen and glucose) to produce ATP.","slow oxidative (SO)",["fast glycolytic (FG)","fast oxidative (FO)","ATPase"],"Slow oxidative (SO) fibers contract relatively slowly and use aerobic respiration (oxygen and glucose) to produce ATP.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282811","f-170ea287c7d9"],["q10-9b5a6971bc38","s10.5-a583448c9459","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nLastly, ____ fibers have fast contractions and primarily use anaerobic glycolysis.","fast glycolytic (FG)",["fast oxidative (FO)","slow oxidative (SO)","myosin"],"Lastly, fast glycolytic (FG) fibers have fast contractions and primarily use anaerobic glycolysis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1282811","f-a6fa213c5f1a"],["q10-d347c700e16b","s10.5-0e793af0b53c","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe speed of contraction is dependent on how quickly myosin’s ____ hydrolyzes ATP to produce cross-bridge action.","ATPase",["glycolysis","fast oxidative (FO)","slow oxidative (SO)"],"The speed of contraction is dependent on how quickly myosin’s ATPase hydrolyzes ATP to produce cross-bridge action.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1905669","f-a956caab43cd"],["q10-9231bd4717c1","s10.5-eb4c57370d23","10.5","Types of Muscle Fibers","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nGlycolytic fibers primarily create ATP through anaerobic ____, which produces less ATP per cycle.","glycolysis",["myosin","ATPase","fast glycolytic (FG)"],"Glycolytic fibers primarily create ATP through anaerobic glycolysis, which produces less ATP per cycle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1905669","f-a6008f64f15b"],["q10-87ad52faa815","s10.6-5cce9f651416","10.6","Endurance Exercise","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nformation of blood capillary networks","angiogenesis",["hypertrophy","sarcoplasm","sarcopenia"],"angiogenesis: formation of blood capillary networks.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2296979","f-05edc2bd62bf"],["q10-8567cbd9256c","s10.6-cc417a206e5c","10.6","Exercise and Muscle Performance","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nage-related muscle atrophy","sarcopenia",["sarcolemma","sarcoplasm","twitch"],"sarcopenia: age-related muscle atrophy.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1584189","f-eb244ad025fc"],["q10-de91e0c18e3a","s10.6-7ed58b35beae","10.6","Exercise and Muscle Performance","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe reverse, when structural proteins are lost and muscle mass decreases, is called ____.","atrophy",["hypertrophy","angiogenesis","sarcopenia"],"The reverse, when structural proteins are lost and muscle mass decreases, is called atrophy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1584189","f-0df92786e0fc"],["q10-05173e6fb8e2","s10.6-f1d02837f944","10.6","Endurance Exercise","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nEndurance exercise can also increase the amount of myoglobin in a cell, as increased ____ increases the need for oxygen.","aerobic respiration",["skeletal muscle","creatine phosphate","muscle tension"],"Endurance exercise can also increase the amount of myoglobin in a cell, as increased aerobic respiration increases the need for oxygen.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364284","f-c47f1f6474d1"],["q10-617418f96c80","s10.6-25b3d6901347","10.6","Endurance Exercise","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMyoglobin is found in the ____ and acts as an oxygen storage supply for the mitochondria.","sarcoplasm",["hypertrophy","sarcolemma","atrophy"],"Myoglobin is found in the sarcoplasm and acts as an oxygen storage supply for the mitochondria.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2364284","f-1763f0c82186"],["q10-53a303a56000","s10.6-5cce9f651416","10.6","Endurance Exercise","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe training can trigger the formation of more extensive capillary networks around the fiber, a process called ____, to supply oxygen and remove metabolic waste.","angiogenesis",["sarcopenia","sarcoplasm","sarcolemma"],"The training can trigger the formation of more extensive capillary networks around the fiber, a process called angiogenesis, to supply oxygen and remove metabolic waste.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2296979","f-a8844a8bbc44"],["q10-7fe834cd5f27","s10.6-41d82176e2d1","10.6","Resistance Exercise","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nExcept for the hypertrophy that follows an increase in the number of sarcomeres and myofibrils in a ____, the cellular changes observed during endurance training do not usually occur with resistance training.","skeletal muscle",["creatine phosphate","aerobic respiration","muscle tension"],"Except for the hypertrophy that follows an increase in the number of sarcomeres and myofibrils in a skeletal muscle, the cellular changes observed during endurance training do not usually occur with resistance training.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1849854","f-d9e47240b00d"],["q10-6cc7377a123d","s10.6-071626c5e7b8","10.6","Resistance Exercise","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCellular damage to muscle fibers that occurs after intense exercise includes damage to the ____ and myofibrils.","sarcolemma",["angiogenesis","hypertrophy","atrophy"],"Cellular damage to muscle fibers that occurs after intense exercise includes damage to the sarcolemma and myofibrils.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2004940","f-fe397eefc2e9"],["q10-d36dd79e6d81","s10.6-633d97e5f326","10.6","Performance-Enhancing Substances","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ provides quick bursts of ATP to muscles in the initial stages of contraction.","creatine phosphate",["aerobic respiration","muscle tension","skeletal muscle"],"Creatine phosphate provides quick bursts of ATP to muscles in the initial stages of contraction.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2240374","f-6da2a18476b6"],["q10-2e8bfee14dbe","s10.6-cc417a206e5c","10.6","Aging and Muscle Tissue","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlthough atrophy due to disuse can often be reversed with exercise, muscle atrophy with age, referred to as ____, is irreversible.","sarcopenia",["sarcolemma","twitch","sarcoplasm"],"Although atrophy due to disuse can often be reversed with exercise, muscle atrophy with age, referred to as sarcopenia, is irreversible.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2267886","f-b3830f7da5d5"],["q10-ecee7af4fcbe","s10.6-ac8bdc808a8f","10.6","Aging and Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere may also be a reduction in the size of motor units, resulting in fewer fibers being stimulated and less ____ being produced.","muscle tension",["aerobic respiration","creatine phosphate","skeletal muscle"],"There may also be a reduction in the size of motor units, resulting in fewer fibers being stimulated and less muscle tension being produced.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2267886","f-9b99831cf8eb"],["q10-d4cb6f605a91","s10.6-cc417a206e5c","10.6","Aging and Muscle Tissue","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ can be delayed to some extent by exercise, as training adds structural proteins and causes cellular changes that can offset the effects of atrophy.","Sarcopenia",["hypertrophy","sarcolemma","sarcoplasm"],"Sarcopenia can be delayed to some extent by exercise, as training adds structural proteins and causes cellular changes that can offset the effects of atrophy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1425445","f-7abfbeef3fa7"],["q10-92368d8f08e8","s10.7-b999aaaaf5e4","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nheart’s ability to control its own contractions","autorhythmicity",["sarcolemma","depolarize","desmosome"],"autorhythmicity: heart’s ability to control its own contractions.","The answer begins with A and has 1 word.","fs-id2254456","f-14a0e38b6db2"],["q10-f656ab914221","s10.7-fe793560b04b","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncell structure that anchors the ends of cardiac muscle fibers to allow contraction to occur","desmosome",["depolarize","sarcolemma","autorhythmicity"],"desmosome: cell structure that anchors the ends of cardiac muscle fibers to allow contraction to occur.","The answer begins with D and has 1 word.","fs-id1389642","f-e80e74280520"],["q10-32969b281f5d","s10.7-4723e51db491","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\npart of the sarcolemma that connects cardiac tissue, and contains gap junctions and desmosomes","intercalated disc",["skeletal muscle","cardiac muscle","action potential"],"intercalated disc: part of the sarcolemma that connects cardiac tissue, and contains gap junctions and desmosomes.","The answer begins with I and has 2 words.","fs-id1978114","f-789122e16bac"],["q10-e31affbebde9","s10.7-a0be188e3a92","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ tissue is only found in the heart.","cardiac muscle",["skeletal muscle","action potential","intercalated disc"],"Cardiac muscle tissue is only found in the heart.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1978114","f-51742c939e06"],["q10-2e4389a4e944","s10.7-a0be188e3a92","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nHighly coordinated contractions of ____ pump blood into the vessels of the circulatory system.","cardiac muscle",["intercalated disc","skeletal muscle","action potential"],"Highly coordinated contractions of cardiac muscle pump blood into the vessels of the circulatory system.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1978114","f-ba698da81a96"],["q10-bf2956ed8338","s10.7-41d82176e2d1","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nCardiac muscle fibers are shorter than ____ fibers and usually contain only one nucleus, which is located in the central region of the cell.","skeletal muscle",["action potential","intercalated disc","depolarize"],"However, cardiac muscle fibers are shorter than skeletal muscle fibers and usually contain only one nucleus, which is located in the central region of the cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1978114","f-997f40fcf1e1"],["q10-e026be16fd8b","s10.7-4723e51db491","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAn ____ allows the cardiac muscle cells to contract in a wave-like pattern so that the heart can work as a pump.","intercalated disc",["skeletal muscle","action potential","sarcolemma"],"An intercalated disc allows the cardiac muscle cells to contract in a wave-like pattern so that the heart can work as a pump.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1978114","f-207f6ae1890c"],["q10-459d8207d77a","s10.7-a0be188e3a92","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIntercalated discs are part of the sarcolemma and contain two structures important in ____ contraction: gap junctions and desmosomes.","cardiac muscle",["intercalated disc","action potential","skeletal muscle"],"Intercalated discs are part of the sarcolemma and contain two structures important in cardiac muscle contraction: gap junctions and desmosomes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1389642","f-b74962f45170"],["q10-8be664715162","s10.7-4723e51db491","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe remainder of the ____ is composed of desmosomes.","intercalated disc",["skeletal muscle","action potential","cardiac muscle"],"The remainder of the intercalated disc is composed of desmosomes.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1389642","f-001c124fc5fe"],["q10-138e5a58e61f","s10.7-a0be188e3a92","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA desmosome is a cell structure that anchors the ends of ____ fibers together so the cells do not pull apart during the stress of individual fibers contracting.","cardiac muscle",["intercalated disc","skeletal muscle","action potential"],"A desmosome is a cell structure that anchors the ends of cardiac muscle fibers together so the cells do not pull apart during the stress of individual fibers contracting.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1389642","f-a2292b1f918f"],["q10-5451e604507f","s10.7-a0be188e3a92","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nContractions of the heart (heartbeats) are controlled by specialized ____ cells called pacemaker cells that directly control heart rate.","cardiac muscle",["intercalated disc","action potential","skeletal muscle"],"Contractions of the heart (heartbeats) are controlled by specialized cardiac muscle cells called pacemaker cells that directly control heart rate.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1853723","f-462b4c52c0a7"],["q10-2849e02ae8ab","s10.7-a0be188e3a92","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlthough ____ cannot be consciously controlled, the pacemaker cells respond to signals from the autonomic nervous system (ANS) to speed up or slow down the heart rate.","cardiac muscle",["intercalated disc","skeletal muscle","action potential"],"Although cardiac muscle cannot be consciously controlled, the pacemaker cells respond to signals from the autonomic nervous system (ANS) to speed up or slow down the heart rate.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1853723","f-a222e1f17ab8"],["q10-c6410ca1c622","s10.7-41d82176e2d1","10.7","Cardiac Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nUnlike ____, a large percentage of the Ca ++ that initiates contraction in cardiac muscles comes from outside the cell rather than from the SR.","skeletal muscle",["cardiac muscle","intercalated disc","action potential"],"Unlike skeletal muscle, a large percentage of the Ca ++ that initiates contraction in cardiac muscles comes from outside the cell rather than from the SR.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1285162","f-0b317b79a91d"],["q10-dba4940d4e2f","s10.8-c851ac313e41","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nregulatory protein that facilitates contraction in smooth muscles","calmodulin",["troponin","varicosity","latch-bridges"],"calmodulin: regulatory protein that facilitates contraction in smooth muscles.","The answer begins with C and has 1 word.","fs-id2176324","f-0696057d1e29"],["q10-854d4d54a98c","s10.8-071fcc14ad6b","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsarcoplasmic structure that attaches to the sarcolemma and shortens the muscle as thin filaments slide past thick filaments","dense body",["stress-relaxation response","synaptic cleft","skeletal muscle"],"dense body: sarcoplasmic structure that attaches to the sarcolemma and shortens the muscle as thin filaments slide past thick filaments.","The answer begins with D and has 2 words.","fs-id2041917","f-13df172d9cfc"],["q10-2f7793a5590e","s10.8-1e82aa9d1c95","10.8","Hyperplasia in Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nprocess in which one cell splits to produce new cells","hyperplasia",["latch-bridges","neurotransmitter","hypertrophy"],"hyperplasia: process in which one cell splits to produce new cells.","The answer begins with H and has 1 word.","fs-id2166530","f-eba3d8797bc4"],["q10-040b9ed36007","s10.8-3e9429e049c7","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsubset of a cross-bridge in which actin and myosin remain locked together","latch-bridges",["troponin","hypertrophy","varicosity"],"latch-bridges: subset of a cross-bridge in which actin and myosin remain locked together.","The answer begins with L and has 1 word.","fs-id1990264","f-79f069e093df"],["q10-0c6246e900f2","s10.8-c462d726ec0d","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\ncell that triggers action potentials in smooth muscle","pacesetter cell",["dense body","muscle tone","skeletal muscle"],"pacesetter cell: cell that triggers action potentials in smooth muscle.","The answer begins with P and has 2 words.","fs-id2080149","f-3d1740e6d497"],["q10-660d32f2882a","s10.8-bb2fa7c3ded8","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nrelaxation of smooth muscle tissue after being stretched","stress-relaxation response",["skeletal muscle","synaptic cleft","dense body"],"stress-relaxation response: relaxation of smooth muscle tissue after being stretched.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2337983","f-7327288087a4"],["q10-155ae59c532d","s10.8-c86eb1edb58f","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nenlargement of neurons that release neurotransmitters into synaptic clefts","varicosity",["calmodulin","hypertrophy","tropomyosin"],"varicosity: enlargement of neurons that release neurotransmitters into synaptic clefts.","The answer begins with V and has 1 word.","fs-id2080149","f-a52cd34a7591"],["q10-d1ba45bcb261","s10.8-ad55270bdc9a","10.8","Smooth Muscle","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nsmooth muscle found in the walls of visceral organs","visceral muscle",["cardiac muscle","skeletal muscle","stress-relaxation response"],"visceral muscle: smooth muscle found in the walls of visceral organs.","The answer begins with V and has 2 words.","fs-id2080149","f-8b15d6950098"],["q10-fcfb7e1894c7","s10.8-a0be188e3a92","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA dense body is analogous to the Z-discs of skeletal and ____ fibers and is fastened to the sarcolemma.","cardiac muscle",["skeletal muscle","stress-relaxation response","muscle tone"],"A dense body is analogous to the Z-discs of skeletal and cardiac muscle fibers and is fastened to the sarcolemma.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2041917","f-153817a3e536"],["q10-cfd7b7d5f65d","s10.8-ca50810dea8c","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIn a ____ fiber, external Ca ++ ions passing through opened calcium channels in the sarcolemma, and additional Ca ++ released from SR, bind to calmodulin.","smooth muscle",["visceral muscle","synaptic cleft","action potential"],"In a smooth muscle fiber, external Ca ++ ions passing through opened calcium channels in the sarcolemma, and additional Ca ++ released from SR, bind to calmodulin.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2176324","f-4b2f0fedca71"],["q10-95582f0e5d68","s10.8-0de11d6c6b9b","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe heads can then attach to ____-binding sites and pull on the thin filaments.","actin",["neurotransmitter","sarcoplasm","varicosity"],"The heads can then attach to actin-binding sites and pull on the thin filaments.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2176324","f-4afc986d26e4"],["q10-d31a442a9eda","s10.8-41d82176e2d1","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlthough smooth muscle contraction relies on the presence of Ca ++ ions, smooth muscle fibers have a much smaller diameter than ____ cells.","skeletal muscle",["dense body","synaptic cleft","action potential"],"Although smooth muscle contraction relies on the presence of Ca ++ ions, smooth muscle fibers have a much smaller diameter than skeletal muscle cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2308688","f-5ec55820c8db"],["q10-8ad2613c715a","s10.8-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.8","Smooth Muscle","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nT-tubules are not required to reach the interior of the cell and therefore not necessary to transmit an ____ deep into the fiber.","action potential",["smooth muscle","synaptic cleft","skeletal muscle"],"T-tubules are not required to reach the interior of the cell and therefore not necessary to transmit an action potential deep into the fiber.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2308688","f-9349e68d7d80"],["q10-3ede1cc07c55","s10.8-2e27fd8e6bd3","10.8","Smooth Muscle","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSmooth muscle fibers have a limited calcium-storing SR but have calcium channels in the sarcolemma (similar to cardiac muscle fibers) that open during the ____ along the sarcolemma.","action potential",["stress-relaxation response","pacesetter cell","visceral muscle"],"Smooth muscle fibers have a limited calcium-storing SR but have calcium channels in the sarcolemma (similar to cardiac muscle fibers) that open during the action potential along the sarcolemma.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2308688","f-948b3c757a8c"],["q10-7b86dc1085fe","s10.8-ca50810dea8c","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe influx of extracellular Ca ++ ions, which diffuse into the sarcoplasm to reach the calmodulin, accounts for most of the Ca ++ that triggers contraction of a ____ cell.","smooth muscle",["visceral muscle","synaptic cleft","stress-relaxation response"],"The influx of extracellular Ca ++ ions, which diffuse into the sarcoplasm to reach the calmodulin, accounts for most of the Ca ++ that triggers contraction of a smooth muscle cell.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2308688","f-b44a726e36a3"],["q10-93fbfcc09d3b","s10.8-0eeb2861f340","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA low concentration of calcium remains in the sarcoplasm to maintain ____.","muscle tone",["visceral muscle","smooth muscle","stress-relaxation response"],"However, a low concentration of calcium remains in the sarcoplasm to maintain muscle tone.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2010798","f-2b6faded703b"],["q10-f326e7f3cea9","s10.8-ca50810dea8c","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSome ____ can also maintain contractions even as Ca ++ is removed and myosin kinase is inactivated/dephosphorylated.","smooth muscle",["dense body","synaptic cleft","muscle tone"],"Some smooth muscle can also maintain contractions even as Ca ++ is removed and myosin kinase is inactivated/dephosphorylated.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1990264","f-810e1b1c6d07"],["q10-756b7f881c3e","s10.8-ca50810dea8c","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThe triggers for ____ contraction include hormones, neural stimulation by the ANS, and local factors.","smooth muscle",["skeletal muscle","muscle tone","cardiac muscle"],"The triggers for smooth muscle contraction include hormones, neural stimulation by the ANS, and local factors.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2337983","f-9f8182d23dac"],["q10-707dec0d9bbd","s10.8-41d82176e2d1","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAxons of neurons in the ANS do not form the highly organized NMJs with smooth muscle, as seen between motor neurons and ____ fibers.","skeletal muscle",["visceral muscle","muscle tone","dense body"],"Axons of neurons in the ANS do not form the highly organized NMJs with smooth muscle, as seen between motor neurons and skeletal muscle fibers.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2080149","f-46d612bac980"],["q10-1e94c44d09f6","s10.8-c462d726ec0d","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ can spontaneously trigger action potentials and contractions in the muscle.","pacesetter cell",["synaptic cleft","dense body","skeletal muscle"],"A pacesetter cell can spontaneously trigger action potentials and contractions in the muscle.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2080149","f-09a02094bbd4"],["q10-ff3141669522","s10.8-bb2fa7c3ded8","10.8","Smooth Muscle","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nBecause the muscle fibers are not constrained by the organization and stretchability limits of sarcomeres, visceral smooth muscle has a ____.","stress-relaxation response",["synaptic cleft","dense body","cardiac muscle"],"Because the muscle fibers are not constrained by the organization and stretchability limits of sarcomeres, visceral smooth muscle has a stress-relaxation response.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1233172","f-28758cebf056"],["q10-f69a148d0b81","s10.8-ca50810dea8c","10.8","Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMultiunit ____ cells rarely possess gap junctions, and thus are not electrically coupled.","smooth muscle",["dense body","muscle tone","visceral muscle"],"Multiunit smooth muscle cells rarely possess gap junctions, and thus are not electrically coupled.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1950516","f-228038bc3060"],["q10-bf6d6212e137","s10.8-a0be188e3a92","10.8","Hyperplasia in Smooth Muscle","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSimilar to skeletal and ____ cells, smooth muscle can undergo hypertrophy to increase in size.","cardiac muscle",["stress-relaxation response","pacesetter cell","skeletal muscle"],"Similar to skeletal and cardiac muscle cells, smooth muscle can undergo hypertrophy to increase in size.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2166530","f-d52f983829bc"],["q10-a3760356288b","s10.9-681fd1005afd","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nreplacement of muscle fibers by scar tissue","fibrosis",["myoblast","somites","myotube"],"fibrosis: replacement of muscle fibers by scar tissue.","The answer begins with F and has 1 word.","fs-id2329008","f-ca6d962608d9"],["q10-55ea60c3ff32","s10.9-e3accbef33fb","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nmuscle-forming stem cell","myoblast",["atrophy","pericyte","sarcolemma"],"myoblast: muscle-forming stem cell.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1959103","f-726c934d9f3f"],["q10-6422ccca881d","s10.9-0b65779164e6","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nfusion of many myoblast cells","myotube",["somites","sarcolemma","pericyte"],"myotube: fusion of many myoblast cells.","The answer begins with M and has 1 word.","fs-id1959103","f-254c154ec9b6"],["q10-9917dcc7fac7","s10.9-36b0124aa871","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstem cell that regenerates smooth muscle cells","pericyte",["fibrosis","myotube","myoblast"],"pericyte: stem cell that regenerates smooth muscle cells.","The answer begins with P and has 1 word.","fs-id1864819","f-80bb986d7f77"],["q10-e7a1e4e2f0f8","s10.9-ab53a217aa1a","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nstem cell that helps to repair muscle cells","satellite cell",["cardiac muscle","smooth muscle","skeletal muscle"],"satellite cell: stem cell that helps to repair muscle cells.","The answer begins with S and has 2 words.","fs-id2329008","f-f86f6778a9cd"],["q10-9e866fe0e4a1","s10.9-f5c711e5cab9","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","foundations","Which term matches this definition?\nblocks of paraxial mesoderm cells","somites",["myotube","myoblast","pericyte"],"somites: blocks of paraxial mesoderm cells.","The answer begins with S and has 1 word.","fs-id1959103","f-541ab139b85f"],["q10-6ce974e2aa48","s10.9-e51c498b65bf","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nMost ____ of the body arises from embryonic mesoderm.","muscle tissue",["satellite cell","cardiac muscle","skeletal muscle"],"Most muscle tissue of the body arises from embryonic mesoderm.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1959103","f-73e5d7f83392"],["q10-3b46503b9a2e","s10.9-f5c711e5cab9","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nParaxial mesodermal cells adjacent to the neural tube form blocks of cells called ____.","somites",["sarcolemma","pericyte","myotube"],"Paraxial mesodermal cells adjacent to the neural tube form blocks of cells called somites.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1959103","f-340e695d19ef"],["q10-f247d209aa2c","s10.9-e3accbef33fb","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nA ____ is a muscle-forming stem cell that migrates to different regions in the body and then fuse(s) to form a syncytium, or myotube.","myoblast",["sarcolemma","pericyte","atrophy"],"A myoblast is a muscle-forming stem cell that migrates to different regions in the body and then fuse(s) to form a syncytium, or myotube.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1959103","f-e79f810ad070"],["q10-5a882e021727","s10.9-ca50810dea8c","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nGap junctions develop in the cardiac and single-unit ____ in the early stages of development.","smooth muscle",["skeletal muscle","satellite cell","cardiac muscle"],"Gap junctions develop in the cardiac and single-unit smooth muscle in the early stages of development.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1605365","f-3cab7860de26"],["q10-dec90fc34b68","s10.9-41d82176e2d1","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAlthough the number of muscle cells is set during development, satellite cells help to repair ____ cells.","skeletal muscle",["satellite cell","smooth muscle","cardiac muscle"],"Although the number of muscle cells is set during development, satellite cells help to repair skeletal muscle cells.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2329008","f-4a9f8ef65f9d"],["q10-cf979a4fd75b","s10.9-681fd1005afd","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nIf a cell is damaged to a greater extent than can be repaired by satellite cells, the muscle fibers are replaced by scar tissue in a process called ____.","fibrosis",["myoblast","sarcolemma","atrophy"],"If a cell is damaged to a greater extent than can be repaired by satellite cells, the muscle fibers are replaced by scar tissue in a process called fibrosis.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2329008","f-ff837fa32ea1"],["q10-f94ebe8d3305","s10.9-ca50810dea8c","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\n____ tissue can regenerate from a type of stem cell called a pericyte, which is found in some small blood vessels.","smooth muscle",["skeletal muscle","satellite cell","cardiac muscle"],"Smooth muscle tissue can regenerate from a type of stem cell called a pericyte, which is found in some small blood vessels.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864819","f-35bce3a764d4"],["q10-3ce77973820a","s10.9-a0be188e3a92","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nPericytes allow smooth muscle cells to regenerate and repair much more readily than skeletal and ____ tissue.","cardiac muscle",["skeletal muscle","satellite cell","myotube"],"Pericytes allow smooth muscle cells to regenerate and repair much more readily than skeletal and cardiac muscle tissue.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864819","f-c63abdefcf5f"],["q10-03723889cf09","s10.9-41d82176e2d1","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nSimilar to ____ tissue, cardiac muscle does not regenerate to a great extent.","skeletal muscle",["smooth muscle","satellite cell","fibrosis"],"Similar to skeletal muscle tissue, cardiac muscle does not regenerate to a great extent.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864819","f-b96da81c284e"],["q10-39a7f0c41919","s10.9-a0be188e3a92","10.9","Development and Regeneration of Muscle Tissue","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nDead ____ tissue is replaced by scar tissue, which cannot contract.","cardiac muscle",["satellite cell","smooth muscle","skeletal muscle"],"Dead cardiac muscle tissue is replaced by scar tissue, which cannot contract.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id1864819","f-cdf230e8cb9a"],["q10-0cf486339ef9","s10.9-7ed58b35beae","10.9","Physical Therapist","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nThere are various causes of ____, including mechanical injury, disease, and age.","atrophy",["myotube","sarcolemma","fibrosis"],"There are various causes of atrophy, including mechanical injury, disease, and age.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2269378","f-563ad823f12c"],["q10-f80c3f91f53f","s10.9-7ed58b35beae","10.9","Physical Therapist","connections","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAfter breaking a limb or undergoing surgery, muscle use is impaired and can lead to disuse ____.","atrophy",["fibrosis","myotube","sarcolemma"],"After breaking a limb or undergoing surgery, muscle use is impaired and can lead to disuse atrophy.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2269378","f-380a65bbf180"],["q10-45e65f7b1011","s10.9-7ed58b35beae","10.9","Physical Therapist","application","Which term correctly completes this statement?\nAge-related muscle loss is also a target of physical therapy, as exercise can reduce the effects of age-related ____ and improve muscle function.","atrophy",["pericyte","myoblast","myotube"],"Age-related muscle loss is also a target of physical therapy, as exercise can reduce the effects of age-related atrophy and improve muscle function.","Identify the structure, process, or substance that fits the role described in the statement.","fs-id2269378","f-798a9136d494"],["q10-edit-fddf8c36da79","s10.2-edit-b29da3994b58","10.2","The Sarcomere","connections","What happens to thick and thin filament lengths as a sarcomere shortens?","The filaments keep their lengths while sliding past one another",["Both thick and thin filaments shorten by the same amount","Only the thick filaments shorten","Only the thin filaments shorten"],"During sliding-filament contraction, actin and myosin filaments overlap more without shortening themselves.","Use the role or mechanism described in the question.","fs-id2141504","f-92dfa83d53a5"]]);
